 # Slice-based and Proxy Geometry Rendering
 
-Also called: Proxy Geometry, Slice-based Volume Rendering, Texture-Based Volume Rendering
 
 ## Overview
-Slice-based and Proxy Geometry Rendering is a direct volume ray casting technique that converts 3D volumetric data into a sequence of 2D polygonal slices (proxy geometry) which are rasterized and composited using standard graphics hardware [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering large volumetric datasets by leveraging the fixed-function pipeline or fragment shaders for alpha blending, avoiding the computational cost of per-pixel ray marching loops [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Texture-Based Volume Rendering](4707-texture-based-volume-rendering.md) by explicitly generating geometric primitives (polygons) for each slice rather than relying solely on texture coordinate generation within a single pass, and from [Direct volume ray casting](19-direct-volume-ray-casting.md) by replacing the continuous sampling integral with discrete, view-dependent or object-aligned polygon rasterization [S1][S2, unofficial].
